{ Learn where the pieces go and the usual order: ...e6, ...Nc6, ...Qc7, ...a6, ...b5 and ...Bb7, often followed by ...Rc8. Add ...Nf6, ...Be7 and castling when the position permits. Keep the d-pawn flexible. Develop first on the side opposite White's castled king. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 { The speedrun's default. Anti-Sicilians can change the moves that follow. } 3. d4 cxd4 { Capture when White must recapture with a piece. If a pawn on c3 can recapture, stop and check: taking may give White a broad centre. } 4. Nxd4 Nc6 { In the main Open Sicilian: knight before queen, queen before a-pawn. } 5. Nc3 Qc7 { If Nb5 attacks the queen, ...Qb8 followed by ...a6 is often available, but check the position. } 6. Be2 a6 { ...a6 prepares ...b5 and controls b5. } 7. O-O b5 { White has castled short, so Black builds on the queenside. } 8. Bf3 Bb7 { ...b5 opens the long diagonal for the bishop. Follow with ...Rc8, ...Nf6, ...Be7 and ...O-O in the order the position allows. } *

[Event "02 Recapture rules on c6"]
[Site "chessbrah Taimanov speedrun"]
