[Black "Black (Taimanov)"]
[Result "*"]
[Orientation "black"]
[ECO "B48"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Taimanov Variation"]
[Variant "Standard"]

{ When the queen is ready on c7 and the move is safe, ...Qxc6 keeps the pawns together. If the queen is not ready, ...bxc6 is often the simple choice, followed by ...d5 and usually ...cxd5 after White exchanges. A bishop recapture, ...dxc6, or an immediate e5 can change the answer, so check the position. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. Be3 a6 7. Be2 b5 8. O-O Bb7 9. Nxc6 { Decide how to recapture before moving automatically. } 9... Qxc6 { The queen is ready, the move is safe, and Black keeps the pawns together. Compare this with ...Bxc6 in the position on the board. } 10. Bf3 Rc8 { Keep pressure on the c-file, but calculate White's e4-e5 break. } *

[Event "03 English Attack, Be3 and Qd2"]
[Site "chessbrah Taimanov speedrun"]
[Date "2024.??.??"]
[Round "-"]
[White "White"]
[Black "Black (Taimanov)"]
[Result "*"]
[Orientation "black"]
[ECO "B48"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Taimanov, English Attack"]
[Variant "Standard"]

{ Against the English Attack, Qd2 usually means White intends to castle long. Bring out the kingside pieces before starting the queenside pawn race. Before White plays g4, ...h5 can slow the pawn storm; after g4, ...h6 can sometimes meet g5 with ...hxg5. The two ideas are not interchangeable. ...d6 gives a knight on f6 the d7 retreat after e5 or g5. Use ...Qa5, ...Bb4 and sometimes ...Bxc3 against the queenside king. Check both knight captures on b5 before playing ...Bxa3. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. Be3 a6 7. Qd2 { Prepare the kingside pieces first because White is likely to castle long. } 7... Nf6 { White must now consider ...Ng4 against the bishop. } 8. f3 (8. O-O-O Ng4 { White castled long without covering g4, so Black can attack the bishop. } 9. Nxc6 Nxe3 10. Qxe3 Qxc6 { The queen recapture keeps the b-pawn free for the attack. } 11. f3 b5 { Start with ...b5-b4; ...a5-a4-a3 may follow. } 12. Kb1 b4 13. Nd5 { The knight is not secure here. } 13... Bc5 { The bishop attacks the queen before White can settle the knight on d5. }) 8... Be7 9. O-O-O b5 10. Nxc6 Qxc6 11. e5 { The KNeres post-game position. White attacks the f6-knight. } 11... b4 { Look for a counter-threat before retreating. ...b4 attacks c3; if the knight moves, d5 becomes available to Black's f6-knight. The move itself is not check. } 12. exf6 { White carries out the original threat. } 12... bxc3 { The pawn attacks the queen on d2. ...cxd2+ is the next threat. } 13. Qxc3 Qxc3 14. bxc3 Bxf6 { The queens come off and Black takes the pawn on f6, reaching a comfortable position. } *

