{ Against Bd3, ...Ne5 attacks the bishop and prepares ...Nc4. The route is strongest when White's queen is on d2, because ...Nc4 can attack queen and bishop. With the queen on e2 it may achieve little; with a white knight on b3, Na5 may answer ...Nc4, so ...b4 can be needed first. After ...Nc4 Bxc4, ...Qxc4 often keeps Black's pawns and c-file in better shape than ...bxc4. Do not exchange on d3 merely to let cxd3 repair White's structure. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. Be3 a6 7. Qd2 Nf6 8. Be2 { This move leaves e4 open to pressure. } 8... Bb4 { Attack e4. } 9. O-O Nxe4 { Black can take the centre pawn. } 10. Qd3 Nxc3 11. bxc3 Be7 { Black has won a pawn and damaged White's structure, but the game still has to be played. } *

