{ In a closed centre, Black's king can sometimes remain on e8; castling short may invite Qg4 or Bh6. ...a5-a4 can stop b3. ...Nd7-c5 attacks a bishop on d3, and ...Ba6 can trade Black's light-squared bishop for a useful defender. Another slow plan is ...Qb6 and ...Na6-b4 against a2. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. Nxc6 bxc6 { Here White has no immediate e5, so the b-pawn recapture is sound. } 7. Bd3 d5 { Use the c6-pawn to support the central break. } 8. O-O Nf6 9. Qe2 Be7 10. e5 { White gains space. } 10... Nd7 { The knight has a useful retreat. If it had to return to g8, Black's move order would have gone wrong. } 11. f4 c5 { Black advances the central pawns. } *

