[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Taimanov, Maroczy Bind"]
[Variant "Standard"]

{ Against the Maroczy, c4 stops the normal ...b5 plan. Use ...Nf6 against e4, ...Bc5 against d4, and ...Qb6 against d4 and b2. After Nxc6, the recapture depends on whether ...Bc5 has been played: in Aman's examples, ...bxc6 is often possible before ...Bc5, while ...dxc6 may be needed afterward so that e5 does not leave the f6-knight without a square. In queenless positions, the king can be active on e7. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. c4 { White controls b5, so change plans. } 5... Nf6 { Attack e4. } 6. Nc3 Bc5 { Attack the d4-knight. } 7. Be3 Qb6 { Attack d4 and b2. } 8. Na4 { White attacks queen and bishop while covering b2. } 8... Bb4+ { The check forces White to solve several problems at once. } 9. Bd2 Qxd4 { The knight on d4 is loose. Take it rather than retreating the queen. } *

