{ Against Bc4, the e6-pawn protects f7 and controls d5. Change the order: pawns before pieces. Play ...a6 and ...b5 before ...Nc6. Use ...c4 only when the bishop has retreated to b3; if it goes to d3, or a pawn on d3 controls c4, return to normal development. Against an early a4, ...b6 and ...Bb7 can develop the bishop without forcing ...b5. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. Bc4 a6 { Pawns first: ...a6 prepares ...b5 and keeps the bishop off b5. } 4. Nc3 b5 { Gain space and attack the bishop. } 5. Bb3 c4 { The bishop must move to a4. It is restricted, not trapped. } *

