{ Against c3, attack the e4-pawn with ...Nf6 before White builds a centre with d4. This applies after 1.e4 c5 2.c3 and after 2.Nf3 e6 3.c3. A Qa4+ fork does not win the c6-knight while Black's d-pawn remains on d7. In these structures, ...b6 and ...Bb7 are often more useful than ...a6 and ...b5. } 1. e4 c5 2. c3 { White intends d4, so do not use the normal Open Sicilian order. } 2... Nf6 { Attack e4. If White plays d4 carelessly, check whether ...Nxe4 wins a pawn. } 3. e5 Nd5 4. c4 { White attacks the knight again. } 4... Nc7 { From c7 the knight supports ...d6, ...b5 and ...Ne6. } 5. d4 cxd4 6. Nf3 d6 { Challenge the e5-pawn. } 7. Bd3 Nc6 8. O-O dxe5 { Remove the advanced pawn while it is safe to do so. } *

