{ Against f4, White's e4-e5 push is stronger and e5 may not be available to Black's knight. This is a clear reason to play ...d6. The knight can sometimes use ...Na5-c4 or ...Nh6-f5 instead. After White weakens g3, ...h5-h4 and ...Nh5-g3 may become useful. In closed positions Black can sometimes walk the king from e8 to g8 instead of castling. } 1. e4 c5 2. f4 e6 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. Bb5 Qc7 { After Bxc6, the queen can recapture and keep the pawns together. } 5. Bxc6 Qxc6 6. d3 d5 7. e5 d4 { Gain space and open the long diagonal. } 8. Nbd2 Ne7 9. Ne4 Nf5 *

