{ Against Nb3 and b3/Bb2 setups, try to keep the dark-squared bishop. Wait until White's b1-knight has committed before rushing ...b5; otherwise White can answer ...b4 with Nbd2 in one move. ...a6 reduces Bb5xc6 ideas, and ...d4 can close the b2-bishop's diagonal. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Nb3 { Do not rush ...Bb7 if White can force a trade of the dark-squared bishop. } 6... Nf6 7. Be3 b5 8. Qf3 Qc7 9. O-O-O d6 { Use ...Nf6, ...Qc7 and ...d6 before deciding where the bishop belongs. } 10. g4 Ne5 { Meet the kingside expansion in the centre. ...b4 may follow; an immediate ...Rb8 is too slow here. } *
